WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.749
When preference tuning is worth it.

2
00:00:02.029 --> 00:00:08.216
For a product team, the decision comes down to the feedback you can collect and the problem you actually have.

3
00:00:08.496 --> 00:00:18.757
Use supervised fine-tuning, not preference tuning, when correct outputs can be written down: extraction, classification, format conversion, the studio project.

4
00:00:19.037 --> 00:00:26.120
Gold labels define “better” more precisely than comparisons would, and SFT is simpler to run and to evaluate.

5
00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:42.656
Consider preference tuning when there are many acceptable outputs, reviewers can reliably pick the better of two, and the supervised model is already on-task but not yet good enough: tone and style, helpfulness of explanations, how cautious or concise to be.

6
00:00:42.936 --> 00:00:54.243
Start from a supervised model (DPO’s reference), collect pairs that reflect real disagreements (two drafts from your current model, one picked by a reviewer), and hold some pairs out.

7
00:00:54.523 --> 00:01:00.624
Consider neither when a better prompt, a better retrieval step, or a different base model would fix the problem.

8
00:01:00.904 --> 00:01:08.115
As week 2 argued, training is the last resort, and preference tuning is the most demanding kind of training to evaluate.

9
00:01:08.395 --> 00:01:23.904
Evaluate preference tuning the way you evaluate anything: held-out pairwise win rate against the supervised model, judged by people or by a judge model you calibrated against people (Studio 3), plus your existing metrics, to catch regressions.

10
00:01:24.184 --> 00:01:32.333
Watch for responses getting longer: length is a well-known way for a preference-tuned model to look better to raters without being better.

11
00:01:32.613 --> 00:01:39.909
And keep Gudibande’s warning from lesson 10 in mind: a model can learn to sound preferred faster than it learns to be right.
